WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.040
Have you ever had a contractor at your house

00:02.040 --> 00:03.840
pointing up at your roof and just, you know,

00:03.840 --> 00:05.760
firing off a bunch of words that sound like

00:05.760 --> 00:07.720
a totally different language?

00:07.720 --> 00:09.720
The step flashing on the rake needs attention

00:09.720 --> 00:10.920
near the ridge.

00:10.920 --> 00:13.560
Yeah, it can make your head spin, right?

00:13.560 --> 00:15.960
Well, today, that's all gonna change.

00:15.960 --> 00:18.120
We are gonna break down the essentials of your roof

00:18.120 --> 00:19.420
so the next time someone's up there,

00:19.420 --> 00:21.420
you'll know exactly what they're talking about.

00:21.420 --> 00:22.920
Let's get into it.

00:22.920 --> 00:24.760
So here's our game plan.

00:24.760 --> 00:26.120
We're gonna build your roofing knowledge

00:26.120 --> 00:29.160
from the ground up, well, I guess from the roof down.

00:29.200 --> 00:31.680
We'll kick things off with the big picture shapes.

00:31.680 --> 00:33.240
Then we'll zoom in on the shingles

00:33.240 --> 00:35.400
and the key terms for all those edges.

00:35.400 --> 00:37.640
We'll even introduce you to the unsung hero

00:37.640 --> 00:38.760
of waterproofing.

00:38.760 --> 00:41.040
And then, yep, we're gonna put you to the test

00:41.040 --> 00:42.280
with a little quiz.

00:42.280 --> 00:44.120
By the time we're done, you'll see why knowing

00:44.120 --> 00:46.640
this stuff is so important.

00:46.640 --> 00:49.280
All right, section one, let's do this.

00:49.280 --> 00:51.600
Before we get into all the nitty gritty details,

00:51.600 --> 00:53.800
we gotta understand the basic structure.

00:53.800 --> 00:55.600
I mean, the overall shape of your roof

00:55.600 --> 00:57.240
is the first thing anybody sees.

00:57.240 --> 00:59.400
And it really sets the stage for everything else

00:59.400 --> 01:01.080
we're about to talk about.

01:01.080 --> 01:05.160
Okay, so first up is an absolute classic, the gable roof.

01:05.160 --> 01:07.440
I promise you, you've seen this a million times.

01:07.440 --> 01:09.280
It's that classic triangular shape

01:09.280 --> 01:11.440
that you'd see in a kid's drawing of a house, right?

01:11.440 --> 01:14.800
It's just two sloping sides that meet at the very top,

01:14.800 --> 01:16.640
creating that perfect peak.

01:16.640 --> 01:21.040
It's simple, it's effective, and it is super common.

01:21.040 --> 01:23.560
Now, on the other hand, you've got a hipped roof.

01:23.560 --> 01:25.840
So instead of just two sloping sides,

01:25.840 --> 01:28.320
this design has slopes on all four sides.

01:28.320 --> 01:30.040
They all angle up towards the top.

01:30.040 --> 01:32.720
And because of that design, it's actually a lot more stable

01:32.720 --> 01:35.780
in high wind areas, like say hurricane zones.

01:35.780 --> 01:39.000
So quick recap, two sloping sides, that's a gable.

01:39.000 --> 01:41.280
Four sloping sides, that's a hipped roof.

01:41.280 --> 01:42.760
Easy enough, right?

01:42.760 --> 01:44.800
Let's move on to section two.

01:44.800 --> 01:46.940
Now that we've got the basic shapes down,

01:46.940 --> 01:48.980
let's talk about what's actually on it.

01:48.980 --> 01:50.600
The material covering your roof

01:50.600 --> 01:52.960
is literally its first line of defense

01:52.960 --> 01:55.520
against sun, rain, wind, you name it.

01:55.520 --> 01:57.800
And while there are a ton of options out there,

01:57.800 --> 02:01.360
a few are way, way more common than the others.

02:01.360 --> 02:05.040
And the undisputed champion, the king of roofing materials,

02:05.040 --> 02:08.720
especially here in the US, is the asphalt shingle.

02:08.720 --> 02:11.880
It's just the perfect combo of durable, affordable,

02:11.880 --> 02:12.840
and versatile.

02:12.840 --> 02:15.780
Plus, it comes in a ton of different styles and colors.

02:15.780 --> 02:19.840
That's exactly why you see it on the huge majority of homes.

02:19.840 --> 02:22.640
But of course, asphalt isn't the only game in town.

02:22.640 --> 02:25.340
You'll also see those really sleek metal roofs,

02:25.380 --> 02:27.860
or maybe some rustic-looking cedar shakes.

02:27.860 --> 02:30.220
And for those flat or low-slope parts of a roof,

02:30.220 --> 02:32.300
maybe over a porch or a garage,

02:32.300 --> 02:35.580
you'll see different materials like rubber or rolled asphalt.

02:35.580 --> 02:37.620
Every single one has its own look,

02:37.620 --> 02:40.940
its own lifespan, and its own price tag.

02:40.940 --> 02:43.040
Okay, here we go, section three.

02:43.040 --> 02:45.300
This is where we really get into the vocabulary.

02:45.300 --> 02:48.100
Seriously, if you can just master these next three terms,

02:48.100 --> 02:50.580
you are gonna be able to follow like 80%

02:50.580 --> 02:52.740
of any conversation about your roof.

02:52.740 --> 02:54.900
These words describe the main lines and edges

02:54.900 --> 02:56.580
of the whole structure.

02:56.580 --> 02:58.980
Let's start from the bottom and work our way up.

02:58.980 --> 03:00.060
The eave.

03:00.060 --> 03:03.020
This is the lower, horizontal edge of your roof.

03:03.020 --> 03:04.220
It's the part that often hangs

03:04.220 --> 03:06.220
just over the walls of the house.

03:06.220 --> 03:07.980
Basically, if you're looking for the gutters,

03:07.980 --> 03:09.940
you're looking at the eave.

03:09.940 --> 03:11.380
Now, if you look at the edge of the roof,

03:11.380 --> 03:13.500
but you follow it up at an angle,

03:13.500 --> 03:14.860
you're looking at the rake.

03:14.860 --> 03:17.040
This is the sloped, diagonal edge

03:17.040 --> 03:19.620
that you see on the side of a gable roof.

03:19.620 --> 03:22.540
So remember, the flat bottom edge is the eave,

03:22.540 --> 03:25.420
and that angled side edge is the rake.

03:25.420 --> 03:27.220
And that brings us to the very top.

03:27.220 --> 03:29.280
The peak, you've got the ridge.

03:29.280 --> 03:32.180
This is that horizontal line right at the tippy top

03:32.180 --> 03:33.940
where the two sloping sides meet.

03:33.940 --> 03:35.060
So let's lock it in.

03:35.060 --> 03:37.860
Eave is at the bottom, rake is the diagonal,

03:37.860 --> 03:39.740
and ridge is at the very top.

03:39.740 --> 03:41.100
You got this.

03:41.100 --> 03:43.380
Okay, on to section four.

03:43.380 --> 03:45.460
Let's talk about one of the most critical,

03:45.460 --> 03:49.220
but honestly, most overlooked parts of any roof.

03:49.220 --> 03:50.480
I love this analogy.

03:50.520 --> 03:53.060
Think of flashing as your roof's raincoat.

03:53.060 --> 03:55.440
It's one and only job is to keep water

03:55.440 --> 03:58.400
out of all the most vulnerable spots.

03:58.400 --> 04:01.440
At its heart, flashing is actually pretty simple.

04:01.440 --> 04:04.600
It's just a thin material, usually it's metal,

04:04.600 --> 04:08.400
and roofer use it to guide water away from tricky areas.

04:08.400 --> 04:10.640
Think about any spot where your roof

04:10.640 --> 04:14.580
bumps into something else, a wall, a chimney, a skylight.

04:14.580 --> 04:16.680
That's a weak spot, and that is exactly

04:16.680 --> 04:18.900
where you're gonna find flashing.

04:18.900 --> 04:21.900
And flashing is not a one-size-fits-all kind of thing.

04:21.900 --> 04:25.420
Nope, there are very specific types for specific jobs.

04:25.420 --> 04:27.180
For example, step flashing.

04:27.180 --> 04:29.880
That gets woven in with the shingles, piece by piece,

04:29.880 --> 04:31.580
right where a roof meets a wall.

04:31.580 --> 04:34.020
Chimney flashing is a whole complex system of metal

04:34.020 --> 04:35.260
that seals around the brick.

04:35.260 --> 04:37.320
Each type is perfectly designed for its job

04:37.320 --> 04:40.000
to make sure everything stays bone dry.

04:40.000 --> 04:42.400
All right, you have been an amazing student.

04:42.400 --> 04:44.720
You've taken in a ton of information.

04:44.720 --> 04:48.120
So it is time for section five, the pop quiz.

04:48.120 --> 04:50.320
Let's see if you can put all that new vocabulary

04:50.320 --> 04:51.380
to good use.

04:51.380 --> 04:52.920
You ready for this?

04:52.920 --> 04:55.400
Okay, question number one, take a look.

04:55.400 --> 04:57.280
We're pointing at the highest horizontal point

04:57.280 --> 05:00.360
of this roof, right where the two big slopes come together.

05:00.360 --> 05:01.440
What do we call that?

05:01.440 --> 05:03.500
I'll give you a second to think about it.

05:03.500 --> 05:07.360
Yes, that's the ridge, awesome, you nailed it.

05:07.360 --> 05:10.520
That's the peak, the very top of your roof.

05:10.520 --> 05:13.320
Question two, this one's a little trickier.

05:13.320 --> 05:15.380
See this L-shaped piece of metal?

05:15.380 --> 05:17.340
It's woven in piece by piece,

05:17.340 --> 05:20.200
right in between the shingles next to that vertical wall.

05:20.200 --> 05:22.480
What kind of flashing is that?

05:22.480 --> 05:25.940
You got it, that is step flashing doing its job,

05:25.940 --> 05:28.780
keeping that super critical spot nice and dry.

05:28.780 --> 05:30.600
Fantastic job.

05:30.600 --> 05:32.140
So this has been fun,

05:32.140 --> 05:35.420
but now let's get to the real payoff here in section six.

05:35.420 --> 05:37.300
Why does any of this even matter?

05:37.300 --> 05:39.960
I mean, this isn't just trivia for a game night.

05:39.960 --> 05:42.440
This is about empowering you in real life situations

05:42.440 --> 05:45.060
that involve your single most valuable asset,

05:45.060 --> 05:46.260
your home.

05:46.260 --> 05:49.720
Look, knowing these terms means you can actually read

05:49.720 --> 05:52.140
an inspection report and understand what those photos

05:52.140 --> 05:52.980
are showing you.

05:52.980 --> 05:54.840
You can look at a confusing insurance estimate

05:54.840 --> 05:56.340
and know what they're talking about.

05:56.340 --> 05:59.460
And maybe most importantly, you can ask a contractor

05:59.460 --> 06:02.640
specific smart questions and know if their answers

06:02.640 --> 06:04.440
actually make sense.

06:04.440 --> 06:07.200
And this is where it gets really, really critical.

06:07.200 --> 06:09.500
When a great contractor is documenting damage

06:09.500 --> 06:12.520
for your insurance claim, they use very specific words.

06:12.540 --> 06:15.080
They might note that a simple repair attempt

06:15.080 --> 06:17.720
caused unavoidable mechanical creasing.

06:17.720 --> 06:20.860
This proves the shingles are old and failed a brittle test.

06:20.860 --> 06:24.260
And all of that together demonstrates the irreparability

06:24.260 --> 06:25.240
of the roof.

06:25.240 --> 06:27.820
Trust me, that precise language is often the key

06:27.820 --> 06:29.780
that gets you a full roof replacement

06:29.780 --> 06:31.940
instead of just a tiny patch.

06:31.940 --> 06:35.740
By understanding these basics, the ridges, the rakes,

06:35.740 --> 06:38.300
the flashing, you've built the foundation.

06:38.300 --> 06:40.860
You are now equipped to handle that complicated world

06:40.880 --> 06:43.360
of inspections and estimates and repairs

06:43.360 --> 06:45.120
with real confidence.

06:45.120 --> 06:47.060
You can protect your investment simply because

06:47.060 --> 06:49.180
you now speak the language.

06:49.180 --> 06:51.400
So I'll just leave you with this one thought.

06:51.400 --> 06:53.680
Next time you get a chance, step outside,

06:53.680 --> 06:55.620
look up at your own home and think about

06:55.620 --> 06:56.920
what you just learned.

06:56.920 --> 06:58.960
What is one question about your roof?

06:58.960 --> 07:00.740
Maybe about its shape or its edges

07:00.740 --> 07:02.120
or where the flashing should be

07:02.120 --> 07:04.120
that you can now answer all by yourself.

